---
/**
 * How the number is made.
 *
 * This page exists because the product's claim is unusual and therefore has to be checkable. Any
 * site can print a green circle; the interesting part here is which fourteen things go in, what
 * each is worth, and what happens when a cheap sensor and an atmospheric model disagree. If that
 * cannot be read in one page, the claim is just branding.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Bar from "../components/Bar.astro";
import { SIGNALS, SITE, abs } from "../lib/site";
import { WEIGHTS } from "../lib/signals";
import { getSitemapCounts } from "../lib/db";

const counts = await getSitemapCounts();

const description =
  "How Air Signal computes outdoor comfort: fourteen environmental signals with fixed weights, " +
  "community sensors reconciled against an atmospheric model, and the quality bar a sensor page " +
  "has to clear.";

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "TechArticle",
    headline: "How the comfort score is computed",
    description,
    url: abs("/how-it-works"),
    author: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE.name, url: SITE.origin },
  },
];

const byWeight = [...SIGNALS].sort((a, b) => WEIGHTS[b.key] - WEIGHTS[a.key]);
---

<Layout title={`How the comfort score works — ${SITE.name}`} description={description} path="/how-it-works" schema={schema}>
  <main class="wrap" style="padding-top:22px" id="main">
    <div class="app">
      <Bar />

      <div style="padding:24px 20px 8px">
        <div class="eyebrow">Method</div>
        <h1 style="font-size:27px;margin:5px 0 0">How the number is made</h1>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-top:12px;font-size:15.5px">
          A person does not go outside for PM2.5. They go out into a combination of heat, wind, UV,
          pollen, sea temperature and daylight — and a single air-quality index cannot say "the air
          is clean but the UV will burn you in twenty minutes", which is the actually useful
          sentence.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="padding:8px 20px 20px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line-soft)">
        <h2 style="margin-top:14px">The fourteen signals, and what each is worth</h2>
        <p style="margin-top:8px;max-width:62ch;font-size:14px">
          Weights sum to 1.00 and are fixed — they live in the Rust core, not in this page. A signal
          with no reading is dropped from the denominator rather than scored zero, so a place with
          no noise sensor is judged on what it actually knows instead of being punished for silence.
        </p>

        <div class="grid-signals" style="margin-top:16px;border-left:1px solid var(--line-soft)">
          {
            byWeight.map((s) => (
              <div class="sig">
                <span class="name">{s.name}</span>
                <div class="reading">
                  <span class="measure num">{(WEIGHTS[s.key] * 100).toFixed(0)} %</span>
                  <span class="pts">{s.key}</span>
                </div>
                <div class="track">
                  <i class="scale-good" style={`width:${(WEIGHTS[s.key] / 0.2) * 100}%`} />
                </div>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>

      <div style="padding:18px 20px 22px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line-soft)">
        <h2>When the sensors and the model disagree</h2>
        <p style="margin-top:8px;max-width:66ch;font-size:14px">
          Community sensors are ground truth: they are real measurements at a real address. But a
          cheap device indoors, next to a road, or with a dying fan reads several times high, and
          every other air map plots that at face value.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;max-width:66ch;font-size:14px">
          So each city's sensors are compared against the atmospheric model, and the model's weight
          falls as the disagreement grows and as more sensors agree with each other. A worked case
          from Moscow: the model said 130 µg/m³, ten devices agreed on 6.7, and the merged answer is
          6.2 — not the average of the two, which would have been 68 and wrong.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;max-width:66ch;font-size:14px">
          Every station page states its own divergence, which is the page no other air map has.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="padding:18px 20px 22px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line-soft)">
        <h2>Which sensor pages are indexed</h2>
        <p style="margin-top:8px;max-width:66ch;font-size:14px">
          Sensor.Community has about nine thousand live devices. Publishing a page per device would
          bury the few hundred that answer a question under a few thousand that do not, so a device
          has to clear four things at once:
        </p>
        <ul style="margin-top:10px;max-width:66ch;font-size:14px;color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.8">
          <li>it reported today;</li>
          <li>it existed in the Sensor.Community archive thirty days ago;</li>
          <li>it sits within 25 km of a named city — or is the closest device that city has, so the
            one sensor covering a stretch of coast keeps its page;</li>
          <li>it is among the eight most complete devices in that city.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="margin-top:10px;max-width:66ch;font-size:14px">
          Today that leaves <span class="num">{counts.stationsIndexable.toLocaleString("en-US")}</span> of
          <span class="num"> {counts.stations.toLocaleString("en-US")}</span>. The rest keep their
          page — their owners want it — but point search engines at their city instead of competing
          with it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div style="padding:18px 20px 22px">
        <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
        <div class="cols" style="margin-top:12px">
          <div class="card">
            <div class="eyebrow">Measured</div>
            <p style="font-size:13.5px">
              <a href="https://sensor.community/">Sensor.Community</a> — volunteer-built particulate
              sensors, ODbL. Readings at or below zero and above 500 µg/m³ are dropped at the
              boundary: they are a broken device, not clean or catastrophic air.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="card">
            <div class="eyebrow">Modelled</div>
            <p style="font-size:13.5px">
              <a href="https://open-meteo.com/">Open-Meteo</a> for weather, air quality and marine;
              <a href="https://earthquake.usgs.gov/">USGS</a> for seismicity;
              <a href="https://www.swpc.noaa.gov/">NOAA SWPC</a> for the planetary K-index. The
              model covers every coordinate on Earth, which is why a city with no sensor still has
              a real page.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p style="margin-top:14px;font-size:13.5px">
          The maths is Rust, compiled to WebAssembly. The same binary runs when the data is
          ingested and again in the edge worker that renders this page, so there is no second
          implementation to drift from the first. Your browser does not compute any of it — it asks
          the same worker, which is both cheaper for you and one fewer place to be wrong. <a href={SITE.repo}>The code is open</a>, and
          <a href={abs("/llms.txt")}>/llms.txt</a> states the same thing in a form an agent can read.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
